vm-6 — Closures + uniform inter-calling (plan-2-bytecode-vm).

Make compiled closures work and make every function kind inter-callable.

WHAT
- Closure conversion: for a local LAMBDA / FLET / LABELS that captures outer lexical
  variables, the analyzer (vm-3) marks captured vars; codegen boxes them (a shared
  cell) or copies them into an UPVALUE vector.  MAKE-CLOSURE tmpl-i builds a closure
  object = { compiled-function template, captured environment (display of frames or
  upvalue vector) }.  LOAD-UPVAL / STORE-UPVAL access captured cells.  Decide box-
  per-mutated-captured-var vs a captured-frame display (display is simpler; box only
  variables that are both captured AND SETQ'd).
- Uniform APPLY: one C entry `bocl_apply(callable, args, nargs, results)` that
  dispatches on the object kind:
    BUILT-IN-FUNCTION -> kernel_apply_built_in
    compiled function/closure -> bocl_vm_run
    tree-walked FUNCTION/CLOSURE -> kernel_apply_function/closure (evale body)
  so bytecode can call built-ins and not-yet-compiled tree-walked functions and vice
  versa, with correct &optional/&rest/&key arg processing (share the existing
  parameter-binding for the tree-walked/kernel sides; the VM binds required args
  fast and falls back to the general binder for &optional/&key).
- This gives INCREMENTAL migration: uncompiled code keeps working; only compiled
  functions run in the VM.

ACCEPTANCE: adder-returning closures, mutable captured counters, mutually-recursive
LABELS, a compiled fn calling a tree-walked fn and a built-in and vice versa;
&optional/&rest/&key on a compiled fn; parity with the tree-walker.

DEPENDS ON: vm-4 (and vm-5 for LABELS control).

====================================================================
PROGRESS (v1.8.8, branch vm) — PART 1: closures + interop DONE.

Compiled closures work; every function kind already inter-calls (CALL/CALL-NAMED go
through kernel_apply, which dispatches compiled -> bytecode_apply, tree-walked ->
evale, built-in -> kernel_apply_built_in — so the "uniform APPLY" interop was already
in place from vm-2; the stack-based fast path is the only piece deferred, below).

- CLOSURE MODEL = a per-activation DISPLAY (not per-variable boxes).  A capturing
  LAMBDA compiles to BC_MAKE_CLOSURE t, which builds a display vector
  [ this frame's locals, ...the current upvalues ] and pairs it with template K[t] in
  a CompiledClosure.  LOAD-LOCAL / STORE-LOCAL depth d>0 read/write display[d-1][i]
  (bytecode.c — the vm-2 stubs are now real).  Because the display holds the enclosing
  LOCALS VECTOR (shared, GC-kept-alive by the closure), captured variables are shared
  and mutable across closures and outlive the enclosing call — no separate boxing.
- CAPTURE DETECTION + PROPAGATION: the compiler-state tracks the max upvalue depth a
  template reaches (directly via a depth>0 ref, OR indirectly — a fn that only relays
  a deeper frame to an inner closure is itself made a closure: when a nested template
  reaches depth N, the enclosing fn bumps its own max-depth to N-1).  max-depth>=1 ->
  emit MAKE-CLOSURE; else the template is a plain PUSH-CONST constant.
- FLET / LABELS: each local fn is built (closure or bare template) and stored in its
  enclosing-frame slot; LABELS mutual recursion works because the siblings share the
  enclosing frame (a fn reads a sibling from frame[slot] via its display).  Visibility
  was already resolved by the analyzer (vm-3).
- Verified: tests/test_misc.c::test_vm_closures — adder-returning closures, distinct
  captured bindings, mutable + shared captured counters, depth-2 nesting, mutual
  LABELS, FLET, and a compiled closure consumed by a tree-walked MAPCAR.  Full suite
  green.

REMAINING for this issue (PART 2): the extended lambda list on a compiled fn —
&optional WITH a non-NIL default / supplied-p, &key, &aux (the VM entry currently does
required + &optional-defaulting-to-NIL + &rest; %VM-CHECK-CORE-LAMBDA-LIST still
errors on the rest).  Plan: a compiled PROLOGUE that fills defaults / matches keywords
into the analyzer's slots (a sentinel for "unsupplied" from the VM entry).  Also the
stack-based fast APPLY + the no-C-recursion trampoline + VM stack-overflow signal, and
genuine BC_TAIL_CALL.
